A measure $
u$ on $\mathbb{X}$ is said to be \emph{$\alpha$-homogeneous} for some $\alpha\in\mathbb{R} \setminus \{0\}$ if  egin{equation} 
u(sB)=s^{-\alpha}
u(B),\qquad B\in\mathfrak{B},\; s>0. \end{equation}

Suppose that Assumption~ef{assumption:U} holds. The following are equivalent for a measure $
u$ on $(\mathbb{X}, \mathfrak{B})$.  egin{itemize} \item[i)] The measure $
u$ is a nontrivial $\alpha$-homogeneous measure with  egin{equation} 
u(U_k)<\infty,\qquad k\in\mathbb{N}\,. \end{equation} \item[ii)] The measure $
u$ is the push-forward of the measure $\pi\otimes	heta_\alpha$ by the map $(x,t)\mapsto tx$, where $\pi$ is a probability measure on $\mathbb{X}$ such that  egin{equation} \int_0^\infty \pi(tU_k)t^{\alpha-1} \, dt < \infty,\qquad k\in\mathbb{N}, \end{equation} and $	heta_\alpha$ is the measure on $\mathbb{R}_{++}$ given by $	heta_\alpha(dt) := \alpha t^{-(\alpha+1)} \, dt$. \item[(iii)] For a probability measure $\pi$ on $\mathbb{X}$ such that \eqref{eq:pi-Sk} holds,  egin{equation} 
u(B) = \alpha \int_0^\infty \pi(t B) \, t^{\alpha-1} \, dt \end{equation} for all $B \in \mathfrak{B}$. \end{itemize}

